<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      Give ProjectDir an upper-case drive letter, so that a build with no changes
      does not recompile anything.

      THE PROBLEM

      Before it compiles a project, the GenerateMSBuildEditorConfigFile target
      (in Roslyn's Microsoft.Managed.Core.targets) writes the file
      obj/<Configuration>/<TargetFramework>/<Project>.GeneratedMSBuildEditorConfig.editorconfig.
      The file has one "build_property.<Name> = <value>" line for each
      CompilerVisibleProperty item, so that analyzers and source generators can
      read those properties. One of them is ProjectDir, the full path of the
      project's directory, for example:

        build_property.ProjectDir = C:\Users\me\dev\monocle\libs\FnUtil\

      The file is rewritten only when its text changes, and it is an input to the
      CoreCompile target. So when its text changes, the compiler runs again for
      that project, even though no source file changed.

      By default, ProjectDir is MSBuildProjectDirectory plus a trailing separator,
      and MSBuildProjectDirectory keeps the casing of the path that MSBuild was
      given. "dotnet build" run from a terminal gives it "C:\..." (upper case).
      But VS Code opens a folder with a lower-case drive letter ("c:\..."), and
      while it has this repo open, the lower-case path reaches the editorconfig
      in two ways:

      - Design-time builds. The C# Dev Kit's build host and the Roslyn language
        server run design-time builds of every project with the "c:\" path. They
        run in the background whenever project outputs change, and so after every
        "dotnet build". A design-time build does not compile, but it does write
        the editorconfig.
      - Shared worker nodes. MSBuild runs a build on worker processes ("nodes"),
        and by default it leaves them running afterwards, so that a later build
        can reuse them. Dev Kit's build host starts nodes of its own, and they
        have this repo's projects cached under "c:\" paths. A "dotnet build" that
        connects to one of those nodes evaluates some of its projects as "c:\...",
        and writes "c:" into their editorconfig files itself.

      So the editorconfig of a project is written sometimes with "C:" and
      sometimes with "c:", and the builds take turns changing it:

        1. "dotnet build" writes one casing and compiles.
        2. A design-time build, or a later build on a shared node, writes the
           other casing into some of the editorconfig files.
        3. The next "dotnet build" finds those files changed, and recompiles
           those projects. Go back to step 2.

      The projects that recompile differ from one build to the next, depending on
      which builds ran in between and which nodes they ran on. Windows paths are
      not case sensitive, so both values name the same directory; only the text
      differs. Outside VS Code, or in a copy of the repo that VS Code does not
      have open, the problem does not occur, even when "dotnet build" is started
      from a "c:\" directory.

      This fix covers the design-time builds. Directory.Build.rsp covers the
      shared nodes: it makes every "dotnet build" in this repo start fresh nodes.
      That matters here because builds on shared nodes also write "c:" into
      files that this fix cannot normalize, such as the NuGet restore outputs
      (project.assets.json, *.nuget.dgspec.json) and SourceLink's
      obj/**/*.sourcelink.json, which is a compiler input.

      THE FIX

      Microsoft.Common.CurrentVersion.targets sets ProjectDir only when it is
      still empty:

        <ProjectDir Condition=" '$(ProjectDir)' == '' ">...</ProjectDir>

      Directory.Build.props is imported before that, so the value set here is
      the one every target sees. It is the default value with the first character
      in upper case: MSBuildProjectDirectory with its first character converted
      by ToUpperInvariant(), plus the trailing directory separator that the default
      value also has. Every build, from any program, now writes the same text. On
      Linux and macOS the first character is "/", which ToUpperInvariant() leaves
      unchanged.

      MSBuildProjectDirectory itself is a reserved property that a project cannot
      set, so this fix cannot cover it. That matters only for projects that use
      the Web SDK, which write it into the editorconfig too. No project here
      does yet, but Directory.Build.targets already deals with it for any that
      is added later.

      The problem is a known one, and Microsoft has no plans to normalize path
      casing:
      - https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/62097 (closed as Won't Fix)
      - https://github.com/dotnet/msbuild/issues/10968 (open)
    -->
    <ProjectDir>$([System.String]::Concat($(MSBuildProjectDirectory.Substring(0,1).ToUpperInvariant()), $(MSBuildProjectDirectory.Substring(1)), $([System.IO.Path]::DirectorySeparatorChar)))</ProjectDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.Text.Json.Serialization.RespectRequiredConstructorParametersDefault" Value="true" />
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.Text.Json.Serialization.RespectNullableAnnotationsDefault" Value="true" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
